.TXT 5 11 169 0
Cg a64.500000,64.500000,967
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
}
{\plain {We have seen that FIR filtering
 may be performed efficiently by the DFT
 method. In some applications the data sequence
 length may too long to be read entirely
 into the computer memory. And even if it
 could be read in, we might wish to make
 use of the output as soon as it can be 
computed. Because we want to exploit the
 computational advantage of the DFT method,
 we are led to consider whether we can process
 a long input in conveniently sized subsequences,
 or}{\i  }{\i0 blocks,}{ that would be processed
 by the DFT method, and then reassembled
 to get the desired filtered output. This
 is possible because the filter impulse 
response is of finite length. The desired
 output is the aperiodic convolution of 
the FIR with the data sequence. }{\ul In
 this Section}{, a}{\ulnone  value yp}{\ulnone 
\dn8 n}{\ulnone  of the periodic convolution
 will be called }{\ul \b0 \i0 correct}{\ulnone 
 if it agrees with the value ya}{\ulnone 
\dn8 n}{\ulnone  of the aperiodic convolution.}}
}
.TXT 26 0 1 0
Cg a64.875000,64.625000,518
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
}
{\plain {Thus the following questions arise.\par 
        How big should the blocks be?\par 
        How should they be formed from the
 data sequence?\par         What size DFT
 should be chosen?\par         How should
 the processed blocks be reassembled?\par 
\par We will discuss two solutions, the 
overlap-add and overlap-save methods, which
 are called}{\i0 \b  }{\i0 \b fast convolution}{
 methods. In order to understand these methods
 we will recall the nature of the periodic
 convolution, as the DFT method computes
 a periodic convolution.}}
}
.TXT 22 -10 170 0
Cg a57.875000,75.625000,70
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
}
{\plain {\b \fs24 Periodic Convolution for
 Unequal Length FIR, Block and DFT}}
}
.TXT 3 10 2 0
Cg a63.625000,63.750000,669
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
}
{\plain {\i0 Consider a FIR \{h}{\dn8 \i0 
n}{\i0 , n=0,...,L-1\}, a signal block \{x}{\dn8 
\i0 n}{\i0 , n=0,...,M-1\} and a DFT defined
 for sequences of length N. }{\i0 \i0  We
 wish to compare the periodic and aperiodic
 convolutions f}{\i0 \i0 \i0 or a given 
set (L,M,N)}{\i0 . We assume that M>=L because
 the filter should be "filled" }{\i0 \i0 
\i0 at least once by the signal block for
 at least one correct output value. Also,
 we assume that N>=M because we want to 
fully utilize the signal block. }{\i0 Thus
 \{h}{\dn8 \i0 n}{\i0 \} and \{x}{\dn8 \i0 
n}{\i0 \} are extended, if necessary, by
 zero padding to length N. We define an 
example to be used for illustration.}}
}
.EQN 18 2 3 0
{0:N}NAME:32
.EQN 0 11 4 0
{0:n}NAME:0;{0:N}NAME-1
.EQN 0 37 5 0
{0:dft}NAME({0:v}NAME):\({0:N}NAME)*{0:icfft}NAME({0:v}NAME)
.EQN 5 -48 6 0
{0:L}NAME:8
.EQN 0 11 7 0
({0:h}NAME)[({0:n}NAME):{0:if}NAME({0:n}NAME<{0:L}NAME,(1)/({0:L}NAME),0)
.EQN 0 37 8 0
{0:H}NAME:{0:dft}NAME({0:h}NAME)
.EQN 7 -48 9 0
{0:M}NAME:28
.EQN 0 11 10 0
({0:x}NAME)[({0:n}NAME):{0:if}NAME({0:n}NAME<{0:M}NAME,{0:cos}NAME((2*{0:\p}NAME)/({0:N}NAME)*{0:e}NAME*{0:n}NAME)+{0:cos}NAME((2*{0:\p}NAME)/({0:N}NAME)*{0:\p}NAME*{0:n}NAME),0)
.EQN 0 37 11 0
{0:X}NAME:{0:dft}NAME({0:x}NAME)
