{\plain {To understand (5.6), consider it
 first for}{ j = 0. It simply assigns the
 sequence xblk}{\fs16 \dn6 n,0}{ to the 
first N positions of w, all the values of
 w}{\fs16 \dn6 n}{ having been initialized
 to zero. Next, when j = 1, the right hand
 side will, for n = 0,...,N-M-1, add w}{\fs16 
\dn6 n+M}{ to xblk}{\fs16 \dn6 n,1}{ and
 assign them to w}{\fs16 \dn6 n+M}{. For
 the remaining n, w}{\fs16 \dn6 n+M}{ is
 0 and the values of xblk}{\fs16 \dn6 n,1}{
 are assigned to w}{\fs16 \dn6 n+M}{. Thus
 the overlap is handled. Similarly for the
 remaining j. So, the reconstruction algorithm
 (5.6) builds up w}{\fs16 \dn6 nn}{ in this
 manner.}}
}
