{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
}
{\plain {Suppose that we have available 
a FFT algorithm that requires (N/2)log}{\fs16 
\dn6 2}{N CO s to compute the DFT of an 
N-length sequence. Assume a data sequence
 of length N}{\fs16 \dn6 g}{ is to be filtered
 with an FIR filter of IR length L. We are
 especially interested in, and therefore
 assume, the case N}{\fs16 \dn6 g}{>>L. 
It is convenient to normalize the number
 of CO s by (Ng L), which therefore gives
 the number of CO s required to compute 
each filter output value, normalized by 
the IR length. Then, e.g., the normalized
 CO s for the direct method is 1. The other
 methods can have normalized CO s less than
 or greater than 1. Note that the block 
size M is to be chosen in the fast convolution
 methods. }}
}
.TXT 20 0 208 0
Cg a65.875000,65.625000,129
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
}
{\plain {Your discussion should include 
a procedure for choosing the most efficient
 method and, as appropriate, the best block
 length.}}
}
